WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.422
Mark each node +1 or −1 for its side.

2
00:00:08.422 --> 00:00:14.156
A same-side edge contributes 0; a crossing edge's squared difference is 4.

3
00:00:14.156 --> 00:00:20.733
So xᵀLx is exactly 4 times the cut: 12 for DE | ABC.

4
00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:29.000
Allow only ±1 and you must try every split. Loosen to real numbers, and it becomes an eigenvector problem.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:37.133
At the same unit length: the split vector gives 2.50, the Fiedler vector only 1.77.

6
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:44.000
λ₂ is the relaxed minimum; the price is an answer that is no longer ±1 and must be rounded.

7
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.467
The simplest rounding: cut by sign, trying only the threshold 0.

8
00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:56.044
It ignores balance: on a star with a heavy leaf, one side can hold a single node.

9
00:00:56.044 --> 00:01:01.978
This course tries all n − 1 thresholds and takes the least inside the window.

10
00:01:02.378 --> 00:01:06.733
Why a balance window? Compare three constraints.

11
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:12.200
Isolating one node cuts at least 6; with no limit, the least is 3.

12
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.667
Inside the 40/60 window it is also 3.

13
00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:22.244
“No constraint always cuts off one node” is wrong; a group can come out too small.

14
00:01:22.644 --> 00:01:27.222
The four-node ring's eigenvalues: 0, 2, 2, 4.

15
00:01:27.222 --> 00:01:33.356
λ₂ = λ₃: the second eigenvector is not unique; any direction is valid.

16
00:01:33.356 --> 00:01:40.378
G01's gap is 6.68, a stable order; the smaller the gap, the more fragile.

17
00:01:40.778 --> 00:01:44.311
Is the vector genuine? Read the residual.

18
00:01:44.311 --> 00:01:51.333
The original's three stored decimals give 0.0044; the live solve gives 10⁻¹⁵.

19
00:01:51.333 --> 00:01:57.600
0.0044 is rounding: fine for display, but never recomputed when the graph changes.

20
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:05.067
Large circuits use sparse iterative methods like Lanczos, for only the smallest eigenpairs.

21
00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:07.533
Six things to keep.

22
00:02:20.533 --> 00:02:25.333
Next: improve the cut by swapping nodes across it.
